WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.083
(light upbeat music)

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:05.463
- What's up, guys?

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:07.720
Michael here in an undisclosed location

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.587
to talk about the movie that dared to ask,

00:00:09.587 --> 00:00:11.867
"What if you met another
version of yourself,

00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:13.250
"and they were kinda a dick?"

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:14.247
Obviously, we're talking about

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:16.580
"Dr. Strange in the
Multiverse of Madness."

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.816
Despite a long history
of trying and failing

00:00:18.816 --> 00:00:20.480
to put the surgeon-turned-Wizard

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:21.330
on the big screen,

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.150
Dr. Strange has been a Marvel favorite

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:24.067
since Benedict Cumberbatch

00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:25.980
brought him to life in 2016.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.130
Since, he's climbed the
ranks of the Avengers

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.950
and sorta saved the world.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.040
And the new film did not disappoint.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.256
It's packed with trippy visuals,

00:00:33.256 --> 00:00:34.770
exciting wizard fights,

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.100
and genuinely moving emotional arcs.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.566
But is something deeper going on

00:00:38.566 --> 00:00:41.210
when Strange and newcomer America Chavez

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:42.560
traverse the multiverse?

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.900
Can Wanda's completely bonkers plan

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.230
to recreate her perfect family

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.020
actually teach us something fundamental

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.410
about the human experience?

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.296
Let's explore in this Wisecrack Quicktake,

00:00:51.296 --> 00:00:53.760
The Philosophy of Dr. Strange.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.770
And, you know, spoilers ahead.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.600
The core of this multiversal madness

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.000
revolves around the
competing supernatural forces

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.840
of Dr. Strange and Wanda,

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.240
who has fully embraced

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.630
her Scarlet Witch identity.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.150
The two have opposite motivations.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:07.620
Wanda wants to steal America's

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.210
multiverse-traveling powers,

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.290
thus killing her.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.800
Strange wants to save

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.350
his new friend at all costs

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.780
and stop Wanda's violent, witchy rampage.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.496
See, Wanda is haunted by dreams

00:01:17.496 --> 00:01:19.040
of the children she created

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:19.873
back in Westview.

00:01:19.873 --> 00:01:21.500
And because dreams are actually

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.640
the experiences of other
"you's" in the multiverse,

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.350
which means a multiverse version of me

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:27.740
regularly pees his pants,

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.100
she's tormented by the fact

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.450
that every other Wanda

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.410
seems to have a
picture-perfect life as a mom.

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:34.670
So she hatches a plan

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.040
to steal America's powers

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.480
to travel to a multiverse

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:38.620
where she has kids.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:39.730
This also means she'd have

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:41.220
to kill another version of herself,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.136
but she seems to have not

00:01:42.136 --> 00:01:44.210
totally thought that part through.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:46.330
What's more, having America's ability

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.730
to go to every iteration of the universe

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:50.090
means that if her kids get sick

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.420
or, say, have trouble getting

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.760
into a top-tier college,

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.790
she can travel around the multiverse

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:56.310
and find novel medicines

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:57.870
or a really great SAT tutor.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.600
And again, kill a version of herself.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:01.850
She tries to persuade Strange

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:02.940
to go along with her plan,

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.010
but he refuses

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:05.366
and rallies all of Kamar-Taj

00:02:05.366 --> 00:02:06.710
to protect America.

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:07.710
Wanda, it seems,

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.580
can outpower just about any living being,

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:13.060
but she can't magic her
way into motherhood.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.330
And then there's Dr. Strange,

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.430
who after arguably saving the world,

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:17.480
still has to sit by

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.263
and watch the love of his life, Christine,

00:02:19.263 --> 00:02:20.820
marry some other guy.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.037
She even reminds him that,

00:02:22.037 --> 00:02:24.780
"You always have to be the
one holding the knife."

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:25.613
That is to say,

00:02:25.613 --> 00:02:27.090
he is a total control freak.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.880
The film opens with an alternate

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:29.830
Strange and America

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:30.940
running from a demon.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:32.060
Having lost their lead,

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.120
Strange steals America's powers,

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.440
which he knows will kill her,

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:36.610
rather than yielding them

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:37.980
to a monstrous force.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.530
He's down to take
control of the situation,

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.600
even if it means doing a murder.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.184
Though luckily the demon murks him

00:02:44.184 --> 00:02:46.010
before he murks America.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:46.843
And throughout the film,

00:02:46.843 --> 00:02:48.830
Strange tries to control everything.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.060
He desperately tries

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:51.320
to stop Wanda's antics,

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:52.690
acts as America's caretaker,

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:54.136
acquires the Book of Vishanti,

00:02:54.136 --> 00:02:54.969
and so on.

00:02:54.969 --> 00:02:56.850
Like Wanda, he even tries out

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.060
the dangerous new sport

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.155
of dreamwalking to control,

00:02:59.155 --> 00:03:00.310
of all things,

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.690
his very own decaying corpse.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:04.970
We've got zombies in the MCU, folks!

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:06.820
But after a magic-induced massacre,

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:08.660
Strange is faced with
the frightening reality

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:09.790
that he might not be able

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:11.360
to control Wanda's fury,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:12.760
or save America.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.280
So here we have an
interesting mirroring effect,

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.230
where our protagonist and antagonist

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.350
actually have the exact same problem.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:20.340
And in case you missed it,

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.010
it's neatly summed up in the trailer

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.940
by Sorcerer Supreme Wong.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.240
- You cannot control everything, Strange.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:27.680
- In contrast, we have America,

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.570
who maintains that she cannot control

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:31.350
her power to travel the multiverse.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.460
It only manifests when she's scared.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:34.700
Unlike Strange and Wanda,

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.365
she's keenly aware that many things

00:03:36.365 --> 00:03:38.100
are out of her control.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:39.610
So, how can we make sense

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:40.740
of Strange and Wanda's

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.270
shared desire for total control?

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.540
Well, with the help of our favorite Danish

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:46.670
walking enthusiast, Soren Kierkegaard.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:47.503
And, guys, I get it,

00:03:47.503 --> 00:03:49.040
I talk about him all the time,

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.750
but I went into an unpayable

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:52.064
amount of grad school debt

00:03:52.064 --> 00:03:53.280
to learn about him

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.440
so I need to milk it

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:55.420
for all it's worth.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.170
In Kierkegaard's book "Repetition,"

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.660
he tells a comedic tale of a young man

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:02.029
who is trying to recreate, or repeat,

00:04:02.029 --> 00:04:03.920
a nice trip he took to Berlin.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.395
Because it made him so happy at the time,

00:04:05.395 --> 00:04:07.760
he goes back and stays at the same place,

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:08.900
visits the same theater,

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.250
and hangs at the same coffee shop.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.720
Still, the initial magic is gone,

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.020
and none of these experiences

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:14.860
are as meaningful

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.140
as they were the first time.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:17.560
And this isn't too dissimilar

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.540
from Wanda's desire to
recreate, or repeat,

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.410
the experience she had as a mother.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:23.243
Though, in fairness,

00:04:23.243 --> 00:04:24.076
Kierkegaard's character

00:04:24.076 --> 00:04:24.909
wasn't trying to kill

00:04:24.909 --> 00:04:26.120
a hip teen with superpowers.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.530
One of the ways that Wanda attempts

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:29.091
to do this is to use dreamwalking

00:04:29.091 --> 00:04:31.260
to literally occupy the body

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.000
of another Wanda in the multiverse,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.920
one who is calmly enjoying

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:35.960
her suburban life

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:36.970
with her two boys.

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:38.150
But during this experience,

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:40.370
our Wanda seems oddly out of sync,

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:41.500
and unable to connect

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.250
with this other version of herself,

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.100
and for that matter,

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.760
with the children she
seemingly loves so much.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:47.950
Kierkegaard describes something

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:49.090
similar in "Repetition."

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:51.185
Eventually, after doing
everything possible

00:04:51.185 --> 00:04:53.030
to repeat his beloved trip,

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:54.520
the young man in Berlin realizes

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.086
that there really isn't such a thing

00:04:56.086 --> 00:04:58.090
as literal repetition.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.400
And in attempting to
repeat the experience,

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.730
a contradiction arises.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:03.990
The conflict between his ideal

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:05.140
for how the trip would feel

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:06.420
and what it actually feels

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:07.300
like in reality.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.350
The attempt at repetition left him

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:10.183
with this feeling

00:05:10.183 --> 00:05:12.700
of contradiction and/or impossibility.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:14.740
And we see this when Scarlet Witch Wanda

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.440
literally comes face-to-face

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.560
with suburban mom Wanda and her kids.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.360
Everything she's done has been motivated

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.420
by wanting to once again experience

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.160
the love she had for her boys.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:25.570
But when she finally sees them again,

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:28.760
they are absolutely
(beep) terrified of her.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:29.593
In this moment,

00:05:29.593 --> 00:05:31.660
she experiences a shattering disconnect

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:33.640
between the ideal of once again

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:34.473
being a mother

00:05:34.473 --> 00:05:36.010
and the reality of these kids

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:37.530
thinking she's super scary.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:38.590
And that's the comic

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:39.423
and, in this case,

00:05:39.423 --> 00:05:42.030
tragic irony of repetition
for Kierkegaard.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:43.524
In trying to repeat experiences,

00:05:43.524 --> 00:05:44.699
we ultimately confront

00:05:44.699 --> 00:05:48.090
the absolute impossibility of repetition.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:50.890
Put differently, in trying
to control our future,

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.570
we realize that total
control is impossible.

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:55.004
Kierkegaard wrote in his journals that,

00:05:55.004 --> 00:05:57.737
"Life can only be understood backwards,

00:05:57.737 --> 00:05:59.460
"but it must be lived forward,"

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:00.700
meaning that we should learn

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.010
from our past experiences

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:04.280
instead of trying to
uncritically repeat them.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.200
Ultimately, Wanda stops trying

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:07.570
to control the world around her,

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:09.700
and instead destroys the Darkhold,

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:10.940
so that nobody might ever

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:13.150
be tempted by its evils again.

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:14.410
Our guy Dr. Strange

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:16.540
has a similar realization in the film,

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:17.650
though he's not trying

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:19.530
to recreate anything, per se.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.660
He still wants things to go his way.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:22.694
This has been his move throughout

00:06:22.694 --> 00:06:24.570
his appearances in the MCU,

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.720
he's kinda a cocky prick.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:27.830
And he's not the only one.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:29.220
Throughout the film, he encounters

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.410
other Stranges from across the multiverse

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.780
whose same hubristic desire for control

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:34.920
led to their downfall.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:36.850
What eventually sets 616 Strange

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:37.860
apart is that he is able

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:39.990
to sacrifice this desire for control,

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.843
namely over whether his lived experience

00:06:41.843 --> 00:06:44.333
matches up with his
ideal vision for things.

00:06:44.333 --> 00:06:45.432
It might also help that

00:06:45.432 --> 00:06:47.963
616 Strange and 818 Christine

00:06:47.963 --> 00:06:49.490
got flirty as hell.

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:50.620
He's eventually able

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:52.340
to cede control to America

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:54.810
when he realizes he can't defeat Wanda,

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:56.500
and for once, he needs to give

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:58.500
the proverbial knife to someone else.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.430
Meanwhile, America has
the opposite experience.

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:02.370
Initially, she feels like

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:03.650
she has no control over

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:05.000
her powers, her reality,

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:05.833
and even whether

00:07:05.833 --> 00:07:07.330
she'll ever see her moms again.

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:09.590
By the end, she realizes that even

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:10.640
if she can't control

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:12.240
what's happening around her,

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.640
she can learn to control

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:15.400
herself and her powers.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:16.640
This is pretty meaningful.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:17.722
Existentialists believe that,

00:07:17.722 --> 00:07:20.062
while we have no control
over the conditions

00:07:20.062 --> 00:07:21.670
in which we exist,

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:24.540
we do have responsibility for ourselves

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:25.500
and our own actions,

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:26.900
for better or worse.

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:28.340
This is why for Jean-Paul Sartre,

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:30.264
responsibility is a direct effect

00:07:30.264 --> 00:07:32.320
of our existential freedom.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:34.350
This tension, between control

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:35.210
and lack thereof,

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:37.459
between wanting to live in the ideal world

00:07:37.459 --> 00:07:38.920
and accepting that we live

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:41.500
in the real, messy, complex world

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:42.370
is nothing new.

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:44.540
It's been essential to western philosophy

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:45.510
ever since some Greeks

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.190
in ancient Birkenstocks invented it.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:48.580
Descartes thought that math,

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:50.440
with a side of God, made existence

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.890
into a nice rational structure,

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:54.500
where everything makes sense

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:56.590
and you know you exist because

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:57.940
you're thinking about existing.

00:07:57.940 --> 00:07:59.220
This made reality something

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:00.440
that was understandable

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:02.440
with a level of mathematical certainty.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:04.020
David Hume, on the other hand,

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:05.660
thought this was dumb

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:06.580
and all of our knowledge

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:08.330
is based on lived experience.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:09.950
What we see is more important

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:10.870
than what we think.

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:12.489
Kant sort of mixed these two together

00:08:12.489 --> 00:08:14.060
to argue that reality

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:15.640
does have a formal structure,

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:16.510
and while we can't see

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:17.900
the causes for everything,

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:19.530
we can know some limited stuff

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:20.640
about how the world works.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:22.260
For example, we can know some

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:24.350
of the fundamental principles of things

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:25.527
like morality and truth

00:08:25.527 --> 00:08:28.020
without seeing inside the mind of God.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:29.630
But my debt-lord Kierkegaard,

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:30.463
on the other hand,

00:08:30.463 --> 00:08:32.060
was interested in how all these attempts

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:33.210
at knowledge affect us

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:34.620
at the individual level

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:36.370
in our actual existence.

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:37.400
And as he saw it,

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:38.610
this fundamental tension

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:40.050
between the ideal world

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:41.160
and the real world,

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:43.070
or ideality and actuality,

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:44.410
can lead to despair.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:45.990
Like the young man in Berlin,

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:47.220
or the Scarlet Witch

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:48.220
who wants to be a mom,

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:49.460
we all have experienced

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:50.530
a contradiction between

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.169
how we want our world to be

00:08:52.169 --> 00:08:54.370
and how our world actually is.

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:56.790
And as Strange, Wanda and America learn,

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:58.070
Kierkegaard thinks the way out

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.820
of this despair is making decisions.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:02.070
And these decisions always

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:04.430
involve an element of risk, or faith,

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:06.790
because there is no way of being certain

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:09.360
that your idea is going to be actualized.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:10.647
Dr. Strange must decide

00:09:10.647 --> 00:09:12.480
to give control to America

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:14.610
and not insist on holding the knife,

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:16.470
or the spooky book of evil spells.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:17.660
Wanda needs to decide

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:18.850
that her plan is evil,

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:20.003
and that to start righting her wrongs,

00:09:20.003 --> 00:09:22.630
she needs to destroy the Darkhold

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:24.320
and, seemingly, herself.

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:26.040
Meanwhile, America decides that

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:27.890
she's capable of controlling her power.

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:29.220
And this decision is accompanied

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:30.120
by the faith that

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:31.610
she'll be able to do so.

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:33.110
It should also be no surprise

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:34.795
that Kierkegaard famously emphasized

00:09:34.795 --> 00:09:37.140
the importance of the "leap of faith."

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:38.330
The paradox of all of this

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:39.440
is that there is power

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:40.273
in giving up control,

00:09:40.273 --> 00:09:42.740
and that the most heroic acts in this film

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:44.350
are precisely those decisions

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:45.380
to cede power,

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:47.090
for the sake of doing what's right.

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:48.470
We can't force our lives

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:49.570
to look like the past,

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:50.970
but we can decide to live

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:52.100
differently in the future.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:53.507
The drive for repetition and control

00:09:53.507 --> 00:09:54.950
can be a prison.

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:56.250
And the way out is deciding

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:57.270
to give up control

00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:58.370
and have faith that the future

00:09:58.370 --> 00:09:59.410
can still work out,

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:01.330
even if you can't be certain about it.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:02.200
But what do you guys think?

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.340
Can "Dr. Strange in the
Multiverse of Madness"

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:05.450
teach us a thing or two

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:06.780
about accepting that we're not

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:07.910
in control of everything?

00:10:07.910 --> 00:10:08.990
Or should we all search

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:10.790
for a secret book of evil magic

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:12.380
so we can make our crush like us?

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.220
Let us know what you
think in the comments.

